Design an Elevator System for a building with multiple floors and multiple elevators.

20 floors
5 elevators
Each elevator can serve all floors
Multiple users can request elevators concurrently
We need to handle hall requests (up/down) and floor requests (destination)


_____________________________



Hall request: User presses UP or DOWN on a floor.
Cabin request: User selects a destination floor inside an elevator.
Assignment: Elevator controller selects the best elevator based on distance and current direction/state.
Goal: Minimize waiting time and avoid unnecessary movement.

_____________________________

Nearest elevator alone isn't always the best choice.

For example:

Elevator A is 2 floors away but moving DOWN
Elevator B is 5 floors away and moving UP toward the user

B may be the better choice.


_____________________________



ElevatorSelectionStrategy

For each eligible elevator:

Ignore elevators in MAINTENANCE / OUT_OF_SERVICE.
If an elevator is already at the requested floor, prefer it.
If elevator is moving toward the requested floor and its direction matches the user's request (UP/DOWN), give it high priority.
Consider distance to the requested floor.
Consider the elevator's current direction.
Consider its existing stops / workload.
If no suitable moving elevator exists, choose an IDLE elevator based on minimum distance.




interface ElevatorSelectionStrategy {
    Elevator selectElevator(
        List<Elevator> elevators,
        int requestedFloor,
        Direction requestedDirection
    );
}

________________________


ElevatorController

Responsible for system-level orchestration:

Receives hall requests
Selects the best elevator
Assigns the request
Manages all elevators



Elevator

Responsible for its own internal behavior:

Maintain current floor
Maintain direction/state
Maintain destination requests
Move toward the next destination
Open/close doors



What data structure would you use to maintain destinations for an elevator moving UP and DOWN?

need two  data structures for different directions
because the ones which are going in upward direction and the ones which are going in downward direction should be different

TreeSet<Integer> upStops;
TreeSet<Integer> downStops;



For example, elevator is at floor 5 and moving UP:

upStops   = [7, 10, 15]
downStops = [2, 3]


It will process:

5 → 7 → 10 → 15 → 3 → 2


the direction determines which set you process first.

Direction.UP
    → process upStops in ascending order
    → when empty, switch to downStops

Direction.DOWN
    → process downStops in descending order
    → when empty, switch to upStops


